Thread 
- Thread is a low-level OS execution unit
- Managed manually
- Heavyweight
- Directly mapped to OS thread

Task
- Task is a higher-level abstraction
- Managed by .NET runtime
- Lightweight
- Usually runs on ThreadPool

Thread = Worker
Task = Work assigned to a worker

Thread - 
OS creates new thread
Thread starts
Runs DoWork
Thread ends

Problems:
	•	expensive to create
	•	difficult lifecycle management
	•	no built-in result handling


Task - 
Task scheduled
ThreadPool picks thread
Work executes
Thread reused

Benefits:
	•	easier programming
	•	automatic scheduling
	•	integrates with async/await

A Thread is a low-level OS execution unit used to run code concurrently, while a Task is a higher-level abstraction 
representing asynchronous work. 
Tasks are scheduled by the .NET TaskScheduler and usually executed on ThreadPool threads, making them more lightweight and efficient. 
Tasks also integrate with async/await and provide better exception handling and result management compared to raw threads.

using System.Threading;
using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
	static void Main(string[] args){
		Thread t = new Thread(Method1);
		t.Start();
		Thread t2 = new Thread(Method2);
		t2.Start();
		Task.Run(()=> Method1());
	}
	
	static void Method1(){
		for(int i =0;i<10;i++)
		{
			Console.WriteLine("Method1");
		}
	}
	
	static void Method2(){
		for(int j =0;j <10;j++){
			Console.WriteLine("Method2");
		}
	}
}
	
